<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Justin II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 520–578</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Justin II was Byzantine emperor from 565 to 578, nephew and successor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a>. He refused payments to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a>, and his reign saw the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombard</a> invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 568 and a renewed war with Persia in which the fortress of Dara fell in 573. After losing his sanity he made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiberius">Tiberius</a> II his co-ruler in 574. Embassies from the Nubian kingdoms reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a> during his reign.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/justin-ii.jpg" alt="Justin II" width="320" height="318" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Artemide Aste · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Solidus_of_Justin_II_(obverse).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Byzantine Empire in the reign of Justinian I. According to the chronicler John of Biclaro, the Makurians accepted Christianity around 569 and sent ivory and a giraffe to the emperor <b>Justin II</b> a few years later, which suggests that they at first followed the Chalcedonian church of Constantinople.</blockquote><a href="/empire/makuria">Makuria · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>…sell silk in Persia, the Sogdian Maniakh persuaded the Turkic ruler Istämi to seek trade with Byzantium, and in 568 he led an embassy to the emperor <b>Justin II</b> in Constantinople; the Byzantine envoy Zemarchus set out for the Turks in 569, as the historian Menander Protector records.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sogdians">Sogdians · Contact &amp; Exchange</a></li><li><blockquote>It was added to the Divine Liturgy under Emperor <b>Justin II</b> (565–578), when the room where the gifts were prepared had been separated from the room where they were consecrated.</blockquote><a href="/songs/cherubikon">Cherubikon (the Cherubic Hymn)</a></li><li><blockquote>During a respite from a severe mental illness, the Eastern Roman emperor <b>Justin II</b> proclaimed his general Tiberius caesar and adopted him as his son.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-7">7 December in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/turkey">Turkey</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=557" aria-label="Constantinople on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5259 700 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Justin II"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5709 970h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=41.01,28.98&amp;year=557">What was here in 557 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
